WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:02.700
The only thing I knew about
wasps before this video

00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.540
was that they are those flying insects that
hover round picnics and sting people.

00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:08.400
That was it. I assumed, naively,

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:11.160
that wasps had no useful role
in the world's ecosystem.

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:13.980
So when someone from the
Oregon Department of Agriculture

00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:16.980
got in touch and said that they
send out taxpayer-funded wasps...

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:18.480
I had some questions.

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:20.220
Like... why?

00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:23.520
- This is pest control at a
ecosystem-wide level.

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:26.160
There are hundreds of thousands
of species of wasp

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:28.800
and only a handful of those
actually sting people.

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:32.940
The vast, vast majority of them
go about their entire life,

00:00:32.940 --> 00:00:34.800
and you never see them or notice them.

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:36.240
- Around the turn of the century,

00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:39.720
the brown marmorated stink bug
arrived in the United States.

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:40.980
It's originally from East Asia,

00:00:40.980 --> 00:00:43.680
but enough of them had been
carried in accidentally by global trade

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:47.160
that a breeding population started
and then exploded.

00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:49.560
Those stink bugs are now found
across most of the continent,

00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:53.400
and they consume crops like
apples, pears, hazelnuts and soybeans,

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:55.920
causing millions of dollars
in damage every year.

00:00:55.920 --> 00:00:57.360
- They are a generalist pest.

00:00:57.360 --> 00:01:00.360
They feed on hundreds of
different plant species,

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:04.440
and they have a great range of
climatic conditions

00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:05.880
that they're perfectly happy
to live in.

00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:09.540
Its populations have reproduced
without anything to check it.

00:01:09.540 --> 00:01:12.600
- So what you want is the
stink bug’s natural enemy:

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:16.860
which is a particular type of wasp,
that's being bred inside this chamber.

00:01:16.860 --> 00:01:19.860
- Parasitoid wasps attack other insects

00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:21.840
and lay their eggs
inside of them to reproduce.

00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:23.340
Sometimes that's an insect egg.

00:01:23.340 --> 00:01:28.080
Sometimes they eat the insect alive,
starting at the least vital organs first.

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:33.900
This is where the inspiration for
the chest-burster from Alien came from.

00:01:33.900 --> 00:01:39.480
We breed the stink bugs because the
parasitoid wasps need them to reproduce in.

00:01:39.480 --> 00:01:43.620
As many stink bug eggs as we have
is about as many wasps as we can produce.

00:01:43.620 --> 00:01:46.500
Last year we produced around 20,000,

00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:49.020
and the year before that
we produced around 17,000.

00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:52.560
- And they are tiny! I didn’t realise
until I got here and saw them

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:55.140
that you could get wasps that
are only about a millimetre long.

00:01:55.140 --> 00:01:58.440
They’re so delicate that the researchers
can't use tweezers to move them around.

00:01:58.440 --> 00:02:01.140
they have to gently suck them up
through chiffon fabric

00:02:01.140 --> 00:02:03.000
using the housing of a ballpoint pen.

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:07.560
But human intervention in ecosystems
doesn't have a great history,

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:09.300
so my next question was: well,

00:02:09.300 --> 00:02:11.640
how do they know what
to release and where?

00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:14.880
- Researchers from USDA
and different universities

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:19.320
went to Asia to find out
what attacks it in Asia,

00:02:19.320 --> 00:02:21.660
and why is it not such
a big problem there.

00:02:21.660 --> 00:02:25.500
They brought Trissolcus japonicus
back over to the United States

00:02:25.500 --> 00:02:27.180
into quarantine facilities,

00:02:27.180 --> 00:02:31.260
tested it on North American species
to see if there were any that

00:02:31.260 --> 00:02:33.780
we didn't test in Asia,
that it could also attack.

00:02:33.780 --> 00:02:36.360
Decades of work go into making sure

00:02:36.360 --> 00:02:39.780
that the wasps that we're
releasing are also safe.

00:02:39.780 --> 00:02:42.960
If we give it brown marmorated
stink bug eggs and any other stink bug,

00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:46.680
it'll choose brown marmorated stink bug
95+% of the time.

00:02:47.220 --> 00:02:50.820
But if you give it just
another stink bug egg mass,

00:02:50.820 --> 00:02:53.100
it will use it if there's
no other choice.

00:02:53.640 --> 00:02:55.620
So we were actually a little
on the fence about

00:02:55.620 --> 00:02:57.240
whether or not we wanted to release it.

00:02:57.900 --> 00:02:59.340
And then it showed up anyways.

00:02:59.340 --> 00:03:02.640
- After years and years of work,
the wasps showed up anyway,

00:03:02.640 --> 00:03:04.710
because every year,
millions of flights land

00:03:04.710 --> 00:03:06.840
and millions of shipping containers
arrive at ports,

00:03:06.840 --> 00:03:08.280
and it's not like those are sterile.

00:03:08.280 --> 00:03:11.760
So, now that the fight is being set up
out in the wild anyway:

00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:14.490
why do they still breed and release these?

00:03:14.490 --> 00:03:17.340
- The parasitoid wasp needs
some time to catch up.

00:03:17.340 --> 00:03:21.840
It's around a millimeter and a half long.
It can't fly very far.

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:25.860
We can drive it a lot further than
it can fly in a single year.

00:03:25.860 --> 00:03:29.100
So we actually get those populations started

00:03:29.100 --> 00:03:32.100
without having to fly across
the entire state of Oregon.

00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:35.460
Normally the growers reach out to
the Department of Agriculture

00:03:35.460 --> 00:03:37.320
and we go out to a site visit.

00:03:37.320 --> 00:03:40.080
Not every site is suitable for these wasps.

00:03:40.080 --> 00:03:46.080
They're actually nectar feeders and pollinators,
so they actually do need other plants present.

00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:51.240
They can provide permanent, long-lasting
pest control for not just growers,

00:03:51.240 --> 00:03:52.980
but the entire ecosystem.

00:03:52.980 --> 00:03:54.540
These do not sting people.

00:03:54.540 --> 00:03:59.940
I have worked with not only this wasp, but other
wasps that are much larger than it

00:03:59.940 --> 00:04:03.060
that are parasitoid wasps,
and I have never been stung,

00:04:03.060 --> 00:04:04.920
and I've been working on these
for over a decade.

